1
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:13,978
You are a warm brick chimney on a roof in Germany, right beside a giant nest of twigs.

2
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:32,322
Look down. The town is still asleep. Up here, the day has already started.

3
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:55,080
Count the little grey puffballs tucked beneath their tall parent. Three stork chicks are waking up.

4
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:20,807
Listen behind you. Big wings are rushing right over your bricks.

5
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:46,031
Now watch straight ahead. Both parents tip their heads all the way back and clack their beaks like wooden castanets.

6
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:03,945
Grown-up storks can barely sing at all, so clattering beaks is how they say welcome home.

7
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:30,456
Here comes breakfast, carried back from the wet meadows down by the river.

8
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:53,053
The parent drops earthworms onto the twigs, and the chicks scramble to grab every bite.

9
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:14,452
Young chicks grow so fast they double their weight in about five days.

10
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:32,096
Between meals, they tuck back into the warm hollow of sticks and listen to the wind.

11
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:51,665
Look at the biggest chick standing up in the sun. Watch those wings open wide.

12
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:14,329
It is still too young to fly, yet every flap makes its chest muscles stronger.

13
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:39,134
Look down to your left now. Cars are rolling out, and sweet warm air drifts up from a bakery.

14
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:12,329
Soon one parent will glide back to the river meadows, while the other guards the roof.

15
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:34,304
This nest has its own live camera — Stork nest, Bad Salzungen, on Digital Live Zoo. Go and see who is home right now.
